{"answer":"The fisher will log the return in the communal log, and the rotating leader will verify and update the record.","revised_policy":"The rotating leader may appoint a session facilitator or the community may elect an education coordinator. The facilitator will be chosen by community consensus during a meeting, and the rotating leader authorizes the facilitator to sign attendance confirmations. The rotating leader may also act as facilitator if no other person is appointed.

Each fisher may take a maximum of 5 kg of fish per trip. If a fisher exceeds this limit on two separate trips within a 30‑day period, the rotating leader has the authority to revoke that fisher’s permission to fish for one week and requires participation in a community education session on sustainable practices. The revocation is recorded in the communal log, the fisher must return the excess fish to the lake, and the fisher’s name is marked ‘revoked for one week’ until the period expires and the education session is completed. The fisher must also record the return of excess fish in the communal log upon returning it; the rotating leader will confirm the entry.

If a fisher refuses or fails to return the excess fish, the revocation will be extended until the fish is returned, and the fisher will be required to provide an equivalent weight of fish in subsequent trips or offer community service equal to the weight of the excess fish. Community service shall be measured by the community service coordinator. For each kilogram of excess fish, the fisher must provide 10 hours of lake maintenance work, which may include cleaning docks, weeding shoreline, repairing nets, or building fish pens. The fisher must schedule and complete these hours within the revocation period. The coordinator will log the service, sign a completion statement, and submit it to the rotating leader. The rotating leader will verify the statement and, upon confirmation, remove the ‘revoked for one week’ marker. The rotating leader will verify the fisher attended the education session by obtaining a signed attendance confirmation from the session facilitator; only after receipt of this confirmation will the rotating leader restore fishing permission. After a fisher is revoked, the session facilitator will provide a signed attendance record to the rotating leader. The rotating leader will check that the record matches the fisher’s name and the date of the session. Upon verification, the rotating leader will remove the ‘revoked for one week’ marker from the communal log and allow the fisher to access gear. No fishing permission will be restored until the facilitator’s confirmation is received. The rotating leader records each fisher’s trips and monitors the 5 kg limit. For any revocation, the leader logs the revocation and informs the fisher. The fisher must return excess fish; if they fail to do so, the leader extends revocation and requires community service. The fisher schedules the required service hours with the community service coordinator. The coordinator logs the weight of excess fish, the required hours, and records the fisher’s completion, signing a statement of completion. The coordinator submits the signed statement to the rotating leader. The leader verifies the statement against the fisher’s name, weight, and date, and if verified, removes the ‘revoked’ marker and restores fishing permission. The leader also verifies attendance at the education session via signed confirmation from the session facilitator, and only after receipt restores permission. All records—trip logs, revocation logs, community service logs, attendance confirmations—are maintained in the communal log for transparency.","revised_operationalization":"The fisher records the return of excess fish in the communal log; the rotating leader confirms the entry before lifting the revocation.","reasoning":"Because transparency and accountability require that the act of returning fish be recorded by the fisher and verified by the rotating leader, ensuring the communal log reflects the true state."}

