{"answer":"We keep a simple ledger at the dock. After each trip I weigh my haul and note the total weight on the ledger, then write that I’ve put 20% of that weight into the bucket. The bucket itself is marked with my name and the date. At the start of the month the council counts the fish in the bucket, checks the ledger, and if the weight recorded for a fisher’s 20% deposit is lower than the 20% that should have gone in, that fisher is the one who didn’t meet the rule. The ledger gives us a clear, unambiguous record of who owes the 10% add‑on.

In this way we don’t have to guess or rely on the council’s memory; each fisher is accountable for his own records and the shared pot keeps its integrity.
","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 8kg per trip and must deposit 20% of their catch into a communal reserve for community use.  At the end of every fishing outing, the fisher splits their haul: 80% is kept for personal use and 20% is deposited into the communal pot at the lake shore.  The pot is a marked bucket placed in a locked wooden chest at the shore.  The community council rotates guard duty and holds the key to the chest.  The pot is inspected by the council at the start of each month; if a fisher fails to deposit the required share, they must add 10% of their next catch to the pot.  The communal reserve is used for shared meals, emergencies, or community projects.","revised_operationalization":"1. After each outing, I weigh my catch and note the total weight on the communal ledger at the dock.  2. I separate 20% of the weight into the marked bucket, label the bucket with my name and the date, and place it in the locked chest on the shore.  3. The council member on duty that day holds the key and ensures the chest is not opened during daylight hours.  4. All fishers may deposit into the bucket at any time after a trip.  5. The chest is inspected at the start of each month by the council; the council counts the fish in each bucket, compares the deposit recorded on the ledger with the required 20%, and if a shortfall is found assigns the 10% add‑on penalty to the fisher whose ledger entry shows the missing deposit.  6. The chest remains on the dock, visible to all, so accessibility is maintained while security is upheld.","reasoning":"Adding a ledger and labeling buckets creates a clear, tamper‑evident record that lets the council identify the exact fisher who omitted the required deposit, without relying on memory or guesswork."}
